\>"],
  ImageRangeCache->{{{0, 160.75}, {131.625, 0}} -> {-4.763, 0.0610758, 
  0.00592597, 0.00592597}}],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Hint:",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " You can click on the picture to resize it and move it around."
}], "NoteWell"],

Cell["\<\
Now you have learnt enough to be able to read the rest of the \
document. Each section is in its own composite cell. Open it to read the \
section.\
\>", "Text",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
  Background->None],

Cell["\<\
To close a cell, double-click on its bracket. It takes a while to \
get used to double-clicking on the cell brackets with precision. Do not worry \
if you click on the wrong thing. Everybody does it.\
\>", "Text",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
  Background->None],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Things to remember from this section:",
    FontFamily->"Helvetica",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[GridBox[{
            {
              StyleBox["notebook",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], \(a\ Mathematica\ document\)},
            {
              StyleBox["cells",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], \(notebook\ building\ blocks\)},
            {
              StyleBox[\(cell\ frames\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              \(indicate\ where\ cells\ start\ and\ end\)},
            {
              StyleBox[\(open/close\ cells\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              \(double\ click\ \ on\ cell\ frame\ to\ open/close\ composite\ 
                cells\)}
            }], TextForm]],
    GridBoxOptions->{ColumnSpacings->2,
    ColumnAlignments->{Left}}]
}], "NoteWell"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
  CounterBox["Section", "frontend"],
  ". Using the Front End"
}], "Section",
  CellTags->"frontend"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This section talks about ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " 3.0 user interface, which is called the ",
  StyleBox["front end",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". The other part of ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica,",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " which computes things, is called ",
  StyleBox["kernel",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". The frontend and the kernel are two different processes \[LongDash] the \
kernel only runs if you try to compute something.  The kernel is what is \
needed to run ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  In summary:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[
    FormBox[GridBox[{
          {
            StyleBox[\(Front\ end\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], \(Mathematica\ user\ interface\)},
          {
            StyleBox["Kernel",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], \(Mathematica\ computing\ engine\)}
          }], TextForm]],
  GridBoxOptions->{ColumnAlignments->{Left}}]], "NoteWell"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Manipulating Cells", "Subsection"],

Cell["There are several things you can do with cells:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[
    FormBox[GridBox[{
          {
            StyleBox[\(Open/Close\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], \(Open\ or\ close\ a\ composite\ cell\)},
          {
            StyleBox[\(Create/\(E dit\)\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            \(Make\ a\ new\ cell\ or\ edit\ an\ existing\ one\)},
          {
            StyleBox["Select",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            \(Select\ a\ cell\ by\ clicking\ once\ on\ its\ cell\ frame\)},
          {
            StyleBox["Copy",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], \(Copy\ a\ cell\ onto\ the\ clipboard\)},
          {
            StyleBox["Cut",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            \(Copy\ onto\ the\ clipboard\ and\ \ remove\ a\ cell\)},
          {
            StyleBox["Paste",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            \(Place\ a\ previously\ copied\ cell\ into\ the\ notebook\)},
          {
            StyleBox["Clear",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], \(Remove\ a\ cell\)},
          {
            StyleBox["Group",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            \(Group\ cells\ into\ a\ composite\ cell\)},
          {
            StyleBox["Ungroup",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            \(Break\ up\ a\ composite\ cell\ into\ its\ components\)},
          {
            StyleBox["Merge",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], \(Merge\ cells\ into\ one\ \ cell\)},
          {
            StyleBox["Divide",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], \(Split\ a\ cell\ into\ two\ cells\)}
          }], TextForm]],
  GridBoxOptions->{ColumnAlignments->{Right, Left}}]], "NoteWell"],

Cell["\<\
Here are exercises and examples for each of these operations:\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Open & Close", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You already know that you can open or close a composite cell by \
double-clicking on its bracket. You can also open or close all cells inside a \
composite cell by selecting it, and choosing menu ",
  StyleBox["Cell:Cell Grouping:Open all Subgroups",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["Cell:Cell Groupoing:Close all Subgroups",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  CellFrame->False]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Create & Edit", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If you move the mouse pointer across a notebook, you will notice that it \
changes between a horizontal and a vertical indicator. Horizontal indicator \
means that the mouse pointer is ",
  StyleBox["inside",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a cell, and the vertical indicator means that it is ",
  StyleBox["between two cells",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If you click on a cell, when you see the vertical indicator, you start \
editing the cell. If you click between two cells, when you see the horizontal \
indicator, a long horizontal line will appear. This is the ",
  StyleBox["input indicator",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and it shows where a new cell will appear if you start typing."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[
    FormBox[GridBox[{
          {
            StyleBox[\(Horizontal\ indicator\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            \(\(mouse\ pointer\ between\ cells\ \[Dash]\ click\ &\)\ type\ to
              \ create\ a\ new\ cell\)},
          {
            StyleBox[\(Vertical\ indicator\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{
                RowBox[{
                "mouse", " ", "pointer", " ", "inside", " ", "a", " ", 
                  "cell", " ", 
                  StyleBox["\[Dash]",
                    FontSize->13], 
                  StyleBox["  ",
                    FontSize->13], "click"}], " ", "&"}], " ", "type", " ", 
              "to", " ", "edit", " ", "the", " ", "cell"}]}
          }], TextForm]],
  GridBoxOptions->{ColumnAlignments->{Right, Left}}]], "NoteWell"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["Pleaze fix all mitsakes in thiis cell.", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
And now, create a cell between cell 1 and cell 2 (it does not \
matter what you type in it, just type something):\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(This\ is\ cell\ 1. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(This\ is\ cell\ 2. \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Hint:",
  StyleBox[
  " When you are done with typing a cell, and want to create another one, you \
do not have to use the mouse. The arrow keys \[UpArrow] and \[DownArrow] move \
the input indicator between cells, too.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "NoteWell",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Select", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
Click on the cell bracket to select a cell.
Click and drag over several cell brackets to select several cells at \
once.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning:",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " There is a difference between ",
  StyleBox["selecting a cell",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["selecting the text inside the cell",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". To select a cell, click on its cell bracket. To select the texts inside \
the cell, click and drag across the text."
}], "NoteWell"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["Select cells 3, 4 and 5 (all at once):", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 1. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 2. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 3. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 4. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 5. \)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Copy, Cut, Paste & Clear", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
If you are not familiar with the concept of cutting & pasting, it \
is best if you ask someone about it. Reading a text that tries to explain \
what it is will not help much, and it is really quite simple.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Before you cut or copy a cell, you must select it by clicking on \
its cell bracket.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "When you paste a cell, there are two cases: If you select a cell, then \
paste from clipboard, you will ",
  StyleBox["replace",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the selected cell. If you click between cells, so that the input \
indicator appears, the pasted cell will appear between cells."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "All four operations, ",
  StyleBox["Cut, Copy, Paste",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["Clear",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", can be activated from the ",
  StyleBox["Edit",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " menu. Since you will use them frequently, it is better to use the \
corresponding keyboard shortcuts."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[
    FormBox[GridBox[{
          {" ", "Macintosh", "Unix"},
          {
            StyleBox["Cut",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[\(\[CloverLeaf]\ x\),
              "Text",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[\(\[AltKey]\ x\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"]},
          {
            StyleBox["Copy",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[\(\[CloverLeaf]\ c\),
              "Text",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[\(\[AltKey]\ c\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"]},
          {
            StyleBox["Paste",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[\(\[CloverLeaf]\ v\),
              "Text",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[\(\[AltKey]\ v\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}
          }], TextForm]]]], "NoteWell"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["Sort the following cells, by cutting and pasting:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 2\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 3\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 5\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 6\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 7\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 4\)], "Input"],

Cell["Good. Now remove cell 5 (just cell 5, not cells 6 and 7).", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Group & Ungroup", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The frontend groups cells automatically if ",
  StyleBox["automatic grouping",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " from menu ",
  StyleBox["Cell:Cell Grouping:Automatic Grouping",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  is on. It will group subsections inside sections, text inside \
subsections, etc. This is probably a good choice for a beginner."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Once you get more experienced, you will want to group cells by yourself. \
Then you can turn on ",
  StyleBox["manual grouping",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " from menu ",
  StyleBox["Cell:Cell Grouping:Manual Grouping",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ": If you switch from manual to automatic grouping, the frontend will \
destroy the groups you made. Switching back to manual grouping will not \
restore the old groups!"
}], "NoteWell"],

Cell["\<\
This notebook was written with automatic grouping, as will all \
notebooks for course notes. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["Turn automatic grouping off.", "Text",
  FormatType->InputForm,
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To group cells into a composite cell, select them and choose menu ",
  StyleBox["Cell:Cell Grouping:Group Cells",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". To ungroup a composite cell, select it and choose menu ",
  StyleBox["Cell:Cell Grouping:Ungroup Cell",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Now, group cells 1, 2 and 3.  Also group cells 4, 5, 6.", "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 1. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 2. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 3. \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Next, add cell 7 to the group of cells 4, 5, 6. Hint:",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " select the group and cell 7, then group them together."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 4. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 5. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 6. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 7. \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Now ungroup cells 4,5,6,7.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text"],

Cell["Finally, turn automatic grouping back on.", "Text",
  FormatType->InputForm,
  FontWeight->"Bold"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Merge & Divide", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If you select cells, and use menu ",
  StyleBox["Cell:Merge Cells",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", they will merge together into one cell. This is different from grouping \
cells into composite cells!"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11],

Cell["Merge cells 1 and 2, and group cells 3, 4 and 5.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 1. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 2. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 3. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 4. \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cell\ 5. \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The opposite of merging is dividing cells. You divide a cell by selecting \
the text inside it and choosing menu ",
  StyleBox["Cell:Divide Cell",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". This is different from ungrouping composite cells!"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Select the text \"brown fox jumped\" in the following cell, then choose ",
  StyleBox["Cell:Divide Cell",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Quick brown fox jumped over the lazy dog.", "Text",
  FontFamily->"Courier",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell["Notice how the cells was divided into three cells.", "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Cell Styles", "Subsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There are different ",
  StyleBox["styles of cells",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " \[LongDash] some contain text, other contain pictures, programs, or \
program output. The style determines the appearance of a cell (font, size, \
alignement, etc.) ."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You can ",
  StyleBox["change the style of a cell",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " by selecting the cell and choosing one of the styles from menu ",
  StyleBox["Format:Style",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You can ",
  StyleBox["specify the style of a newly created cell",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " by selecting one of the styles from menu ",
  StyleBox["Format:Style",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " just before you start typing the text of the new cell."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If you do not specify a style, the newly created cell will have the ",
  StyleBox["Input",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " style."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is important that you use the correct cell style. The most common \
mistake that a beginner makes is to type everything in the ",
  StyleBox["Input",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " style. The input style is only supposed to be used for typing commands \
for the kernel."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Create a cell in ",
  StyleBox["Input",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " style that says \"2 + 2\".  Then create a new cell in",
  StyleBox["Text",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " style that says \"I have just created an input cell that said 2+2\"."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Follow these steps:\n\[Bullet] Click between two cells to indicate where \
the new cell will be.\n\[Bullet] Type \"2 + 2\".\n\[Bullet] Press the down \
arrow \[DownArrow] to move out of the cell (you will see the input \
indicator).\n\[Bullet] Select ",
  StyleBox["Format:Style:Text",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " from the menu.\n\[Bullet] Type \"I have just created an input cell that \
said 2+2.\""
}], "Text"],

Cell[" Here is what the result should look like:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2 + 2\)], "Input"],

Cell["I have just created an input cell that said 2+2.", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Typing Text, Formul\[AE] and Fancy Symbols", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Text Style, Font and Size", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You can control the appearance of text by changing its size, font and \
style from menu ",
  StyleBox["Format",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".\nOften you do not have to change font style manually, though, because \
cell styles already define the appearance of fonts."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["Create a cell that looks just like the following one:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is plain text (style Text).\n",
  StyleBox["This is bold text.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\n",
  StyleBox["This is italic text.",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "\n",
  StyleBox["This is bold and italic.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "\n",
  StyleBox["This is red text on green background.",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0],
    Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Formulas and Mathematical Symbols", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "When you type text in a notebook, you are either in ",
  StyleBox["text mode",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["formula mode",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". Text mode is used for typing ordinary text. Formula mode is used for \
typing special symbols, such as Greek letters and integral signs, and \
complicated formulas, such as fractions and square roots."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[
    FormBox[GridBox[{
          {
            StyleBox[\(Text\ mode\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            \(Used\ for\ typing\ text\ in\ a\ text\ cell\)},
          {
            StyleBox[\(Formula\ mode\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            \(Used\ for\ typing\ input\ cells\ and\ inline\ formulas\ in\ text
              \ \(cells . \)\)}
          }], TextForm]],
  GridBoxOptions->{ColumnAlignments->{Right, Left}}]], "NoteWell"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "When you type text, you can switch to formula mode by pressing ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[ControlKey]\ 9\)]],
  " and end it with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[ControlKey]\ 0\)]],
  ". In formula mode, you can type special symbols with the sequence ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{"\[EscapeKey]", 
            StyleBox["code",
              FontSlant->"Italic"], "\[EscapeKey]"}]], TextForm]]],
  ". For example, type ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[EscapeKey]a\[EscapeKey]\)]],
  " for the Greek letter ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[Alpha]\)]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Solve the equation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[Phi]\^2 - \[Phi] - 1 = 0\)]],
  ". Follow these instructions:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "1) Click between two cells so that the input indicator appears.\n2) Type: \
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        StyleBox[
          \(\[EscapeKey]f\[EscapeKey]\ \[ControlKey]6\ 2\ 
                  \[ControlKey]\[SpaceKey]\  - \[EscapeKey]f\[EscapeKey]\  - 
                1 == 0 // Solve\),
          "Input",
          FontFamily->"Courier"], TextForm]]],
  ".\n3) Type ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[ShiftKey]\[ReturnKey]\)]],
  " to pass the command to the kernel. The kernel may take a while to start \
up. Be patient."
}], "Text"],

Cell["The result should look like this:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\[Phi]\^2 - \[Phi] - 1 == 0\  // \ Solve\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Create a Text cell, and type the text \"If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[Alpha] = 1\)]],
  " then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[Alpha]\^2 = 1\)]],
  ".\" Follow these instructions:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "1) Click between two cells so that the input indicator appears.\n2) Select \
from menu ",
  StyleBox["Format:Style:Text",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", or type ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[CloverLeaf]\ 7\)]],
  " on Macintosh, or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[AltKey]\ 7\)]],
  " on Unix. This tells the frontend that the new cell should be a text cell, \
rather than an input cell.\n3) Type: ",
  StyleBox["If ",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm
      \`\[ControlKey]9\ \[EscapeKey]a\[EscapeKey]\  = 1\ \[ControlKey]0\)],
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" then ",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm
      \`\[ControlKey]9\ \[EscapeKey]a\[EscapeKey]\ \[ControlKey]6\ 2\ 
          \[ControlKey]\[SpaceKey]\  = 1  \[ControlKey]0\)],
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["The result should look like this:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[Alpha] = 1\)]],
  " then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[Alpha]\^2 = 1\)]],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Palettes", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There are hundreds of mathematical symbols and it is impossible to \
memorize all ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{"\[EscapeKey]", 
            StyleBox["code",
              FontSlant->"Italic"], "\[EscapeKey]"}]], TextForm]]],
  " sequences. Instead, you can use ",
  StyleBox["palettes ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "which can be activated from menu ",
  StyleBox["File:Palettes",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". A palette is a table of buttons. By pressing the buttons you can insert \
symbols and mathematical expressions into your text."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[
    FormBox[GridBox[{
          {
            StyleBox["BasicInput",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            \(A\ good\ palette\ for\ a\ \(beginner . \)\)},
          {
            StyleBox["AlgebraicManipulation",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            \(This\ palette\ is\ used\ for\ doing\ basic\ \(algebra . \)\)},
          {
            StyleBox["BasicTypesetting",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            \(Use\ this\ palette\ for\ typesetting\ complicated\ 
              \(formulas . \)\)}
          }], TextForm]],
  GridBoxOptions->{ColumnAlignments->{Right, Left}}]], "NoteWell"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Create a Text cell, and type the text \"If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[Alpha] = 1\)]],
  " then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[Alpha]\^2 = 1\)]],
  ".\" Use palettes. Follow these instructions:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "1) Activate the palette ",
  StyleBox["BasicInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " from menu ",
  StyleBox["File:Palettes",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "2) Click between two cells so that the input indicator appears.\n3) Select \
from menu ",
  StyleBox["Format:Style:Text",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", or type ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[CloverLeaf]\ 7\)]],
  " on Macintosh, or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[AltKey]\ 7\)]],
  " on Unix. This tells the frontend that the new cell should be a text cell, \
rather than an input cell.\n3) Type: ",
  StyleBox["If ",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\(\[ControlKey]9\ \[Alpha] = 1\ \[ControlKey]0\ \)\)],
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" then ",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm
      \`\[ControlKey]9\ \[SelectionPlaceholder]\^\[Placeholder]\ \[Alpha]\ 
          \[TabKey]\ 2  \[ControlKey]\[SpaceKey]\  = 1  \[ControlKey]0\)],
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". Use the palette for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[Alpha]\)]],
  " and the power ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[SelectionPlaceholder]\^\[Placeholder]\)]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["The result should look like this:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[Alpha] = 1\)]],
  " then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[Alpha]\^2 = 1\)]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If you look at the ",
  StyleBox["BasicInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " palette, you will notice that math formulas contain empty and full boxes, \
\[SelectionPlaceholder] and \[Placeholder]. These are ",
  StyleBox["placeholders",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and you should fill them with text or formulas. You can move between \
placeholders with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[TabKey]\)]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[
"If you select a piece of formula or text, and press a button on a palette, \
the selected text will fill the full placeholder \[SelectionPlaceholder]."], 
  "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Use the ",
  StyleBox["BasicInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " palette to change the following formula"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[
    \(TextForm
    \`\(a\^2 + b\^2 + c\^2\)\/3 \[GreaterEqual] 
      a\[CenterDot]b\[CenterDot]c\)]]], "Text",
  TextAlignment->Center],

Cell["to the formula", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[
    \(TextForm
    \`\@\(\(a\^2 + b\^2 + c\^2\)\/3\) \[GreaterEqual] 
      \@\(a\[CenterDot]b\[CenterDot]c\)\%3\)]]], "Text",
  TextAlignment->Center],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["Typeset the following formula:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\[Integral]\(1\/\(1 + x\^2\)\) \[DifferentialD]x \[Equal] 
      ArcTan[x] + C\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Hint",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ":",
  StyleBox[" Type ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        StyleBox[\(\[EscapeKey] == \[EscapeKey]\),
          FontFamily->"Courier"], TextForm]]],
  StyleBox[" instead of ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\` == \)]],
  ". ",
  StyleBox["It looks better.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If you press ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm\`\[ShiftKey]\[ReturnKey]\)]],
  " the kernel will evaluate the integral for you. Mathematica does not know \
that indefinite integrals are determined only up to a constant."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Hyperlinks", "Subsection"],

Cell["\<\
Mathematica 3.0 has hyperlinks. Hyperlinks are active pieces of \
text. When you click on them, they take you somewhere. If you have ever used \
the World Wide Web, you know about hyperlinks.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In this notebook you will not learn how to make your own hyperlinks. You \
should know, however, that they exist. Usually they apprear as blue \
underlined text. This is ",
  ButtonBox["a hyperlink example",
    ButtonData:>"1.3.7",
    ButtonStyle->"MainBookLink"],
  ", which will take you to the help system.  We will be using the help \
system in finding examples of how to run ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Help System", "Subsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Mathematica 3.0 has an extensive help system. It can be envoked from menu \
",
  StyleBox["Help:Help...",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
Activate the help system and spend 5 minutes examining how it works \
and what is in it.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If you would like information about a specific kernel command or function, \
you can type the function name and choose menu ",
  StyleBox["Help:Find in Help...",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Some Mathematica function names are very long. Use menu ",
  StyleBox["Input:Complete Selection",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for automatic name completion. The following exercise illustrates how \
this is done."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Suppose you want to type ",
  StyleBox["StieltjesGamma", "Input"],
  ". Instead of typing everything, just type the first few characters (try ",
  StyleBox["Sti", "Input"],
  ") , then choose menu ",
  StyleBox["Input:Complete Selection",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", or press ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        StyleBox[\(\[CloverLeaf]\ k\),
          FontFamily->"Courier",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], TextForm]]],
  " on Macintosh, or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        StyleBox[\(\[AltKey]\ k\),
          FontFamily->"Courier",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], TextForm]]],
  " on Unix. A list of all names starting with  ",
  StyleBox["Sti", "Input"],
  " appears, and you can click on the one you want."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
  CounterBox["Section", "kernel"],
  ". Running ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " "
}], "Section",
  CellTags->"kernel"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Introduction", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Some advice: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If you read the Front end section without taking a break, take one ",
  StyleBox["now",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  \nWhat is coming is much much worse \[LongDash] in the sense that you \
will have more to remember."
}], "Text",
  FontSize->18,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 0, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["kernel",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is Mathematica's brain. Think of it as a big programmable calculator. It \
has more than a thousand built-in functions, and many more are provided in \
add-ons (formerally called packages)."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox[
  "The usual way the kernel is activated is to select or create an ",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14],
  StyleBox["input cell",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[
  ", and then to push the enter key.  If the kernel has not been started \
before, then it will take a little time for it to \"wake up\".  Once the \
kernel is running, smaller calculations usually take very little time before \
the answer appears in the ",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14],
  StyleBox["output cell",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[". ",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14]
}], "Text",
  FormatType->InputForm,
  FontSize->12],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox[
  "Make an input cell for yourself following this text cell.  Type in ",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14],
  StyleBox[" x - 3",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[
  " and cube the whole polynomial.  Then follow it by the post-position \
command ",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14],
  StyleBox["//Expand",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[".  Now run the cell and see what happens.",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14]
}], "Text",
  FormatType->InputForm],

Cell["Delete this cell and make your own.", "Input",
  FormatType->InputForm],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox[
"Go to the Apple menu (File menu on UNIX versions) and create a new notebook. \
 Make some input cells and run them.  Other examples of what to do can be \
found in the following subsections.  You can also work those examples in this \
notebook.",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSize->14]], "Text",
  FormatType->InputForm],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exercise:",
  FontFamily->"Helvetica",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text",
  FontSize->11,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox[
  "After working over the examples in the following subsections, go to the ",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14],
  StyleBox["Help Browser",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" and then click the \n\t\t",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14],
  StyleBox["\"Getting Started/Demos\"",
    FontFamily->"Monaco",
    FontSize->12,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" \nbutton.  Work through the sections that interet you in the ",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14],
  StyleBox["\"Tour of ",
    FontFamily->"Monaco",
    FontSize->12,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontFamily->"Monaco",
    FontSize->12,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["\"",
    FontFamily->"Monaco",
    FontSize->12,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[".  T",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->12],
  StyleBox["hen click the \n\t\t",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14],
  StyleBox["\"Other Information\"",
    FontFamily->"Monaco",
    FontSize->12,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" \nbutton.  Work through the sections that interet you in the ",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->14],
  StyleBox["\"Menu Commands\"",
    FontFamily->"Monaco",
    FontSize->12,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontFamily->"Times",
    FontSize->12]
}], "Text",
  FormatType->InputForm]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Numbers", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Big numbers", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " computes integers to an arbitrary number of digits:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[OutputFormData["\<\
2^100\
\>", "\<\
 100
2\
\>"], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To find out ",
  StyleBox["how many digits",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " an integer has, rewrite it in ",
  StyleBox["scientific notation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell["N[%]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, the power ",
  StyleBox["2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["100",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  " has ",
  StyleBox["31",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " digits.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is another large number.  Remember the ",
  StyleBox["Factorial[n] ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is by definition the product of all the integers from ",
  StyleBox["1 ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "to ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", that is,  ",
  StyleBox["1 x 2 x 3 x 4 . . . (n-2) x (n-1) x n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  It can also be written ",
  StyleBox["n ! ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["500!", "Input"],

Cell["N[%]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This number has ",
  StyleBox["1135 ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "digits.  An interesting question (which we cannot solve here) is how to \
count (by means of a formula) the number of ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "s at the end of such a number."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Little numbers", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Here is a fairly small number:", "Text"],

Cell["2^-100", "Input"],

Cell["Here is its decimal value:", "Text"],

Cell["N[%]", "Input"],

Cell["Here is how to see more decimal places:", "Text"],

Cell["N[2^-100, 30]", "Input"],

Cell["Here is another small number:", "Text"],

Cell["N[1/(E^9 +1),20]", "Input"],

Cell["ScientificForm[%]", "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Arithmetic operations", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If you use ",
  StyleBox[" any ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "decimals, the whole answer cones out as a ",
  StyleBox["decimal number",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell["(15.7 + 98.6)^2 / 55", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If you use ",
  StyleBox["only fractions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (and sums, differences, products, quotients, and integer exponents), then \
you get a fraction:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["(157/10 + 986/10)^2 / 55", "Input"],

Cell["N[%]", "Input"],

Cell["ScientificForm[%]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If you use ",
  StyleBox["fractional exponents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it is your own fault:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
2^(1/3)

N[%]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
2^(-1/3)

N[%]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
(-2)^(-1/4)

N[%]\
\>", "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Inequalities", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Which of the two interesting numbers in this expression is ",
  StyleBox["larger",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?"
}], "Text"],

Cell["E^Pi - Pi^E", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " leaves the result in ",
  StyleBox["symbolic form",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and we have to ask for a numerical evaluation:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["N[%]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, since this is positive, ",
  StyleBox["E",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["Pi ",
    FontSize->11,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  "is larger.  Here is another way to do the comparison:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["E^Pi > Pi^E", "Input",
  FormatType->InputForm],

Cell["Try some examples of inequalities of your own.", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Random numbers", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " also has a random number generator built in, which is useful for making \
up test cases (or perhaps even playing games).  Here is a random integer:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["Random[Integer,13]", "Input"],

Cell["Or you can change the range:", "Text"],

Cell["Random[Integer,{-10,12}]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Or here is a random choice from thr real interval ",
  StyleBox["[0, 1]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell["Random[]", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Run these input cells several times so you can see that each run \
makes another choice.\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["How many miles in a light year?", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Try to explain this computation:", "Text"],

Cell["answer = 60 60 24 (365 + 1/4) 186000", "Input"],

Cell["This can be put into scientific notation:", "Text"],

Cell["N[answer]", "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" A check with dictionary definitions", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
The definitions here were copied from the new electronic version of \
 the Random House Dictionary.\
\>", "NoteWell",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["light",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\n      ",
  StyleBox["1.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " something that makes things visible or affords illumination:  All colors \
depend on light. \n      ",
  StyleBox["2.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["Physics",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "\n          ",
  StyleBox["a.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Also called luminous energy, radiant energy, electromagnetic radiation to \
which the organs of sight react, ranging in wavelength from about 400 to 700 \
nm  and propagated at a speed of 186,282 mi./sec (299,972  km/sec) , \
considered variously as a wave, corpuscular, or  quantum phenomenon. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["light-year",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\n      ",
  StyleBox["1.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["Astron",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". the distance traversed by light in one mean solar year, about 5.88 \
trillion mi. (9.46 trillion km) : used as a unit  in measuring stellar \
distances. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
We should do a revised calculation with the more accurate speed of \
light:\
\>", "NoteWell",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
answer

newanswer = 60 60 24 (365 + 1/4) 186282

N[newanswer]

N[newanswer - answer]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "In an input cell by writing commands on separate lines with blank lines \
inbetween we get the results of separate computations, displayed in a way \
that makes comparisons easy to read."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
By the way, the number of days in a year is a rough approximation.  \
Can you find a better approximation?\
\>", "NoteWell",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A polynomial", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["An example", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " stand for a certain polynomial:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["p = x^7 - x^5 - x^2 - 29;", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " then remembers ",
  StyleBox["p ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "in symbolic form:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
p

p^3

Expand[%]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note the ",
  StyleBox["order",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the terms.  "
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Evaluation", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If you want to ",
  StyleBox["evaluate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "the polynomial ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " at a number, you can do the ",
  StyleBox["ReplaceAll",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " command:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
p

ReplaceAll[ p, x -> 1 ]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
ReplaceAll is used so often, that there is a simple abbreviation \
for it:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["p /. x -> 3", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The replacement rule can also be written in curly braces (in fact, \
there can be whole list of rules):\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
p /.  x -> -1.5
p /. {x -> -1.5}
\
\>", "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Plotting the curve", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we can see a graph of the polynomial as a function of ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell["Plot[p,{x,-3,3}];", "Input"],

Cell["A part is missing, but that can be fixed:", "Text"],

Cell["Plot[p,{x,-3,3}, PlotRange -> {-2000,2000}];", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now some detail is missing, but you can look at parts of the graph \
as you wish.\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Making some substitutions", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Here is a symbolic substitution:", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
p /. {x -> (1 - y)^2}

Expand[%]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["Try some other substitutions into the polynomial.", "Text",
  FormatType->InputForm,
  FontFamily->"Times"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Some other functions", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "These are just random examples illustrating some ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " features."
}], "NoteWell",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A list of numbers", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Lists will be used over and over again to show multiple data and \
answers to computations.  Here is an example:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["alist = {1,9,9,6}", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The identifier ",
  StyleBox["alist",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " has now been assigned a value, and we can call it up for other \
calculations:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["Length[alist]", "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A range of numbers", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There are many special lists that are handy to have.  ",
  StyleBox["Range",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " gives ",
  StyleBox["succesive",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " numbers."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Range[-10, 10]", "Input"],

Cell["Try it with other bounds.", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Mapping the Sin function across the list", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Often a function needs to be evaluated at each of the entries in a list.  \
This is accomplished by using ",
  StyleBox["Map",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Map[Sin,Range[-10,10]]", "Input"],

Cell["?Map", "Input"],

Cell["The first list above is thus just the same as:", "Text"],

Cell["Sin /@ Range[-10,10]", "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Finding the numerical values", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We note that ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " has left the values of the ",
  StyleBox["Sin",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " function in symbolic form.  To see the actual numerical values \
\[LongDash] approximations thereto \[LongDash] use the ",
  StyleBox["N ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "function."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
N[%] 

% // TableForm\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And by using ",
  StyleBox["TableForm ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "we could see the values even better."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Graphing the values", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["The list just calculated can also be exhibited graphically.", "Text"],

Cell["ListPlot[%];", "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Another way of making a graph", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["And the continuous function can also be ploted.", "Text"],

Cell["Plot[Sin[x], {x,-10,10}];", "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
